WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.560
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:12.300
Today is going to be a really fun day&nbsp;
where we do this, and this, and this.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:18.060
In a previous episode, we talked about creating&nbsp;
virtual characters with a skeletal system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:26.280
adding more than 300 muscles and teaching&nbsp;
them to use these muscles to kick, jump,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:34.440
move around and perform other realistic human&nbsp;
movements. You see the activated muscles with red.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:40.920
I am loving the idea, which, turns out,&nbsp;
comes with lots of really interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:46.920
corollaries. For instance, this simulation&nbsp;
realistically portrays how increasing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:51.840
amount of weight to be lifted changes&nbsp;
what muscles are being trained during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:58.380
a workout. These agents also learned to&nbsp;
jump really high and you can see a drastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:04.020
difference between the movement required&nbsp;
for a mediocre jump and an amazing one.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:12.300
And now, scientists at NVIDIA had a crazy idea.&nbsp;
They said, what if we take a similar model, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:18.300
ask it to learn to walk from scratch.&nbsp;
Now that is indeed a crazy idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:24.960
because they proposed a model that is&nbsp;
driven by over 150 muscle-tendon units,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:29.940
and is thus very difficult to&nbsp;
control. So, let’s see how it went.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:40.440
First, it started to…umm…hello? Well, A+ for&nbsp;
effort, but unfortunately, this is not a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:47.460
start. But don’t despair! A little later, it&nbsp;
learned to…well, fall in a different direction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:54.960
however, at least some learning is hopefully&nbsp;
happening. Look. I wouldn’t say that it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:01.740
finally taken the first step, but at least it&nbsp;
is attempting to take a first step. Is that good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:10.320
news? Let’s see! Oh yes, yes it is! Because a&nbsp;
little later, it learned to jog. This concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:17.760
really works! And, if we wait for a bit longer,&nbsp;
we see that it learned to run as well. Fantastic!

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:24.960
Now, let’s have a closer look and see if the&nbsp;
colors of the muscles indeed show us which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:32.100
ones are activated at a given moment. And that’s&nbsp;
right! When slowing the footage down, we see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:38.460
difficulty of the problem - and that is, different&nbsp;
tendons need to be moved every single moment.

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:44.520
So, while we look at this technique learning&nbsp;
other tasks, we ask one of the most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:52.380
questions here, and that is, how fast did it learn&nbsp;
to run? It had to control a 150 different tendons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.380 --> 00:03:00.360
continuously over time, without falling. So how&nbsp;
long did it take? Did it take days? And now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:07.920
hold on to your papers, because it hasn’t taken&nbsp;
days. It only takes minutes. After starting out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:15.480
like this, by the 17-minute mark, it has learned&nbsp;
so much that it could jog. How amazing is that?

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:22.200
And that is one of the key value propositions of&nbsp;
this paper. It can not only teach this AI agent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:30.420
difficult tasks, but it can also learn up to&nbsp;
15 to 17-times faster than previous techniques.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:38.700
That is absolutely amazing. Bravo! So, it seems&nbsp;
that we now have learning-based algorithms that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:44.760
could teach even a complex, muscle-actuated&nbsp;
robot to walk. What a time to be alive!

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:46.200
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

